---
import EnglishHeader from '../../../components/EnglishHeader.astro';
import EnglishFooter from '../../../components/EnglishFooter.astro';
import { englishStories } from '../../../data/english';

const copy = {
  'circuit-de-france-1942': { year:'1942', label:'Racing and society · Occupied France', title:'The Tour the occupation wanted', intro:'The Circuit de France was launched as a six-stage substitute race in occupied France. On a map it resembled the Tour. In practice, it showed how much was missing.', alt:'Illustration of a peloton on a worn European road during the shortages of the 1940s', caption:'A contemporary interpretation of a peloton under the scarce conditions of the 1940s. AI-generated illustration — not a documentary image of the Circuit de France. Illustration: The Evolution of Bikes.', sections:[['A Tour de France without the Tour','The Tour de France was not held during the German occupation. In 1942, sports journalist Jean Leulliot and the newspaper La France socialiste instead attempted to stage the Circuit de France. It began on 28 September and ended on 4 October after six stages through both the occupied and the so-called free zone.'],['A race with political baggage','The newspaper behind the race collaborated with the occupying power, and the event could be presented as evidence of order and normality. That did not make every rider a political actor. But the organiser, the permissions and the coverage cannot be separated from the circumstances in which the race took place.'],['A calendar without fuel or free movement','Transport, petrol, spare parts and food were rationed. Borders and checkpoints made planning difficult, and several leading riders and teams stayed away. The Circuit de France was therefore neither the Tour under a new name nor a normal international stage race.'],['François Neuville won','Belgian François Neuville finished as overall winner. The result is clear, but the race’s main significance lies elsewhere. This was its only edition; plans for a repeat in 1943 came to nothing.'],['What the absence revealed','The Circuit de France showed that a route and a field do not by themselves create a Tour de France. The race also required a newspaper, an audience, supplies, freedom of movement and a degree of public trust. In 1942, each existed only in a limited form. It was not enough.']], sources:[['Hugh Dauncey: French Cycling — A Social and Cultural History','https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/gdc/gdcebookspublic/20/16/44/94/98/2016449498/2016449498.pdf'],['Tour de France: 1940, the Tour that was not held','https://www.letour.fr/en/news/2020/1940-the-tour-that-wasnt-4-10/1283547']] },
  'gino-bartali-kurer-under-krigen': { year:'1943–1944', label:'The people behind cycling · Second World War', title:"Bartali's training rides had another purpose", intro:'Gino Bartali was known for training over long distances. During the German occupation of Italy, that familiar habit made him useful as a courier for a rescue network.', alt:'Gino Bartali after the Tour of Lombardy in 1936', caption:'Gino Bartali in 1936, before the war interrupted the major international races. Photograph: Agence Meurisse/Bibliothèque nationale de France/Wikimedia Commons, public domain.', sections:[['A famous man on familiar roads','After the German occupation of northern and central Italy in September 1943, Bartali worked as a courier for a network around Cardinal Elia Dalla Costa and Rabbi Nathan Cassuto. His long journeys between places including Florence and Assisi looked like what the public already knew him for: a professional rider in training.'],['Documents between cities','Yad Vashem’s investigation documents that Bartali carried false papers and other material between members of the network. Sources describe different hiding places on the bicycle, so the precise location should not be made the story’s single certain detail. His role as a courier is firmly established.'],['The bicycle as an explanation','When stopped, Bartali could point to his training and his famous name. Witness accounts say that he asked officials not to touch the bicycle because its adjustment was vital to his sport. The explanation worked precisely because a professional racing bicycle really was a carefully fitted working tool.'],['Part of a larger network','Bartali did not act alone. Priests, Jewish helpers, forgers, families and couriers each carried out part of the work. The often-repeated figure of more than 800 people saved should therefore not simply be credited to Bartali personally. His contribution was real; it was also collective.'],['A story he did not sell','After the war, Bartali said little publicly about this work. In 2013, Yad Vashem recognised him as Righteous Among the Nations. The recognition rests on testimony and his documented role, not on a need to turn one famous rider into the network’s only hero.']], sources:[['Yad Vashem: Gino Bartali, Righteous Among the Nations','https://wwv.yadvashem.org/yv/en/exhibitions/righteous-sportsmen/bartali.asp'],['United States Holocaust Memorial Museum: Gino Bartali','https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gino-bartali']] },
  'fausto-coppi-fra-krigsfange-til-comeback': { year:'1943–1949', label:'Riders of the period · War and return', title:'From prisoner of war to Giro–Tour winner', intro:'Fausto Coppi had already won the Giro when he was sent to North Africa and captured. When the races returned, the second part of his career began as well.', alt:'Fausto Coppi at the summit of the Ghisallo during the 1957 Tour of Lombardy', caption:'Fausto Coppi on the Ghisallo in 1957. The image is later than the events described, but shows the rider who came to define post-war cycle racing. Photograph: unknown/Wikimedia Commons, public domain.', sections:[['A Giro winner before call-up','Coppi won the 1940 Giro d’Italia at the age of 20. A few days after the race ended, Italy entered the war. His breakthrough therefore did not begin a normal run of international seasons, but several years of military service, interrupted racing and uncertainty.'],['To North Africa','In 1943, Coppi was sent to Tunisia with the Italian army. British forces captured him in April near Mateur and Medjez el-Bab. In captivity he was first a soldier and prisoner of war, not the public champion Italian newspapers had made him.'],['Back through Italy','Coppi was later brought back to Italy with the Allied advance. A surviving letter from November 1944 places him in Caserta. After the war he could race again, but the calendar, teams and supplies had to be reassembled just as the riders did.'],['The victories returned quickly','In 1946, Coppi won Milan–San Remo after a long solo ride. He won the Giro in 1947 and, in 1949, both the Giro d’Italia and Tour de France. He became the first rider to win the two major stage races in the same season.'],['Not an unbroken line of talent','Coppi’s results can look like a straight line from young Giro winner to the post-war Campionissimo. The war cut a large gap through that line. His return is therefore not only a story of talent and modern training, but of a professional career that had to begin again.']], sources:[['Giro d’Italia: historical archive 1933–1947','https://archivio.giroditalia.it/eng/editions/1933-1947/'],['Federazione fra le Società Filateliche Italiane: Coppi, war and surviving correspondence','https://expo.fsfi.it/vastophil2023/exhibits/06PoloWHY2MUdW.pdf']] },
  'jean-robic-vender-touren-1947': { year:'1947', label:'Races and great moments · Tour de France', title:'Robic took yellow on the final day', intro:'Jean Robic had not worn the yellow jersey for a single day. When the final stage began, he was third. On the road to Paris, he overturned the entire classification.', alt:'Jean Robic photographed after winning the 1947 Tour de France', caption:'Jean Robic after his victory in the 1947 Tour de France. Photograph: Anefo/Nationaal Archief, CC0.', sections:[['The Tour returns','The Tour de France was held again in 1947 after a seven-year absence. Pierre Brambilla led before the final stage from Caen to Paris. René Vietto was second and Jean Robic began the day in third. Normally, the road to Paris was not where the overall race was overturned.'],['The attack after Rouen','Robic attacked after Rouen and Édouard Fachleitner joined him. Brambilla could not close the gap. By the time the group reached the Parc des Princes, Robic had gained more than the time he needed and moved into first place in the final classification.'],['Yellow on the podium, not on the road','He thus won the Tour without previously wearing the yellow jersey during the race. It is a peculiar detail, but it should not obscure the achievement: the overall lead was attacked and lost on the final day.'],['An agreement on the road','Later accounts describe agreements between Robic and Fachleitner involving assistance and prize money. The exact words and sums vary. Their cooperation in the attack belongs to the course of the race; every detail of the verbal bargain cannot be treated as a contemporary transcript.'],['The lead bottle came later','Robic is also linked to a story about carrying a heavy bottle filled with lead on mountain descents. That episode is normally associated with the 1953 Tour and does not explain his 1947 victory. He won this day by attacking while the race was still open.']], sources:[['Tour de France: Robic turns the 1947 Tour on its head','https://www.letour.fr/en/news/2024/finish-with-a-capital-f-parc-des-princes-the-home-of-cycling-ii-iv/1316959']] },
  'campagnolo-gran-sport-parallelogram': { year:'1951', label:'Technology · Gear shifting', title:'When gear shifting finally became modern', intro:'Gran Sport made shifting a movement of the derailleur rather than the rear wheel. The modern principle was not new, but Campagnolo placed it at the centre of the Italian racing bicycle.', alt:'Early-1950s Giamé racing bicycle with period gear equipment', caption:'The collection’s early-1950s Giamé shows the kind of light steel bicycle for which the new derailleurs were developed. This bicycle is not documented as having Campagnolo Gran Sport.', sections:[['Before the wheel could stay in place','Cambio Corsa and the Paris–Roubaix gear allowed the rider to shift while moving, but the rear axle still had to be released and the wheel moved in the frame. It demanded both balance and mechanical sensitivity.'],['The parallelogram','Gran Sport moved the chain guide sideways through an articulated parallelogram. A spring kept the chain tensioned while two pulley wheels guided it between sprockets. The rear wheel could now remain securely fixed.'],['Not the first derailleur','Campagnolo did not invent the parallelogram principle. Other manufacturers had already developed related solutions. Its importance lay in a robust, prestigious model closely associated with the fine Italian racing bicycle.'],['A lever on the down tube','Shifting was still not indexed. The rider reached for a friction lever on the down tube and found the quiet chain position by feel. It was modern beside the earlier rod system, but still demanded practice.'],['A form that endured','Gran Sport pointed towards decades of rear derailleurs. Materials, sprocket counts and controls changed, but the basic parallelogram idea remained.']], sources:[['Campagnolo: company history','https://www.campagnolo.com/us-en/company/history/'],['The Evolution of Bikes: Cambio Corsa','https://teob.dk/en/components/campagnolo-cambio-corsa/']] },
  'roger-walkowiak-tour-1956': { year:'1956', label:'Races and great moments · Tour de France', title:'The Tour winner nobody expected', intro:'Roger Walkowiak won no stage in the 1956 Tour. He won the race because the favourites first underestimated his breakaway and later could not drop him.', alt:'Tour de France riders receiving musettes during the 1958 race', caption:'A feed zone during the 1958 Tour de France. The image shows everyday race life shortly after Walkowiak’s victory, but neither Walkowiak nor the 1956 Tour. Photograph: Nationaal Archief, no known restrictions.', sections:[['The regional team','Walkowiak was not selected for the French national team. He rode for Nord-Est–Centre and began without the attention attached to the race’s greatest names.'],['The breakaway was given room','On stage seven, he joined a large break that the favourites allowed to gain too much time. He took the yellow jersey, lost it again and still remained close in the classification.'],['The decisive defence','In the Alps he showed that the advantage was not merely a gift. He followed the strongest riders, regained the lead four days before Paris and denied his rivals the chance to correct their early misjudgement.'],['No stage victory','Winning without a stage made the story difficult to sell as conventional heroism. The phrase “à la Walko” was later used for a surprising or supposedly lucky victory.'],['More than luck','A breakaway can provide time. It cannot by itself defend that time over mountains and through a three-week race. Walkowiak’s victory was unexpected, but not accidental.']], sources:[['Tour de France: One last breakaway for Walko','https://www.letour.fr/en/news/2017/one-last-breakaway-for-walko/1263971']] },
  'charly-gaul-monte-bondone-1956': { year:'1956', label:'Races and great moments · Giro d’Italia', title:'When the snow broke the Giro peloton', intro:'Rain turned to snow on the 242-kilometre stage from Merano to Monte Bondone. Charly Gaul attacked early and finished the day as stage winner and new man in pink.', alt:'Charly Gaul at the team presentation before the 1958 Tour de France', caption:'Charly Gaul stands in the centre at the team presentation before the 1958 Tour de France. The photograph was taken two years after Monte Bondone. Photograph: Harry Pot/Anefo, CC0.', sections:[['Four passes before the finish','The stage crossed Costalunga, Rolle, Gobbera and Brocon before finishing on Monte Bondone. Cold, rain and wind turned the opening hours into an elimination race.'],['Gaul attacks','Charly Gaul began the day in eleventh place, more than sixteen minutes behind Pasquale Fornara. He attacked as the weather worsened and riders disappeared from the field.'],['Snow on Bondone','On the final climb, rain became a snowstorm. Fornara’s directeur sportif withdrew him out of concern for his health. Gaul continued through the cold and won the stage by almost eight minutes.'],['Close to hypothermia','At the finish, Gaul was so badly affected that helpers had to get him off the bicycle and into warmth. The hypothermia should not be treated as decoration: the stage showed how little safety margin separated racing from serious harm.'],['From eleventh to victory','The stage gave Gaul the pink jersey, which he kept to Milan. Bondone endured as one of the Giro’s clearest images of a person pushed to the limit.']], sources:[['Giro d’Italia: Monte Bondone 1956','https://www.giroditalia.it/en/monte-bondone/'],['Giro d’Italia: memories from Monte Bondone','https://www.giroditalia.it/news/ricordi-dal-monte-bondone-1956-luomo-portato-ai-suoi-limiti/']] },
  'tour-start-amsterdam-1954': { year:'1954', label:'Society and the times · Tour de France', title:'When the Tour left France', intro:'The Tour had crossed national borders for years, but its start belonged to France. In 1954, the race began in another country for the first time.', alt:'The Tour de France peloton passing spectators in the Netherlands in 1954', caption:'The peloton on Zandvoortselaan during the Tour’s first Grand Départ outside France in 1954. Photograph: Nationaal Archief, no known restrictions.', sections:[['Amsterdam before Paris','On 8 July 1954, the Tour de France began in Amsterdam. It was the race’s first Grand Départ outside France, although the route had crossed national borders many times before.'],['A Dutch opening day','The first stage ran to Brasschaat in Belgium. Wout Wagtmans won and became the first Dutch rider in yellow after a Tour start on home soil.'],['A European race','Post-war Europe still carried the ruptures of the conflict. Amsterdam cannot simply be called a reconciliation project, but the start showed a race increasingly presenting itself as a shared European event.'],['Cities became participants','A foreign start gave the host city spectators, press coverage and international attention. The model later became a permanent part of the Tour’s geographical and economic expansion.'],['A new beginning','Amsterdam did not change the bicycle. The city changed the idea of where the Tour could begin.']], sources:[['Tour de France: Grands Départs','https://www.letour.fr/en/the-race/grands-departs']] },
  'fausto-coppi-la-bomba': { year:'1952', label:'Nutrition, training and science · Doping history', title:'When “necessary” meant almost always', intro:'Fausto Coppi’s answer about “la bomba” became one of cycling’s most frequently repeated doping remarks. It belongs to an age when use was known but effective controls barely existed.', alt:'Fausto Coppi during the 1957 Tour of Lombardy', caption:'Fausto Coppi on the Ghisallo in 1957. The photograph is later than the interview. Photograph: unknown/Wikimedia Commons; free-use status recorded in the project image archive.', sections:[['A direct question','In a 1952 RAI interview, Coppi was asked whether riders took “la bomba”, the peloton’s term for amphetamine-based stimulants. He answered without the distance created by later rules.'],['Almost always','Asked when it was necessary, he replied in the familiar version: practically all the time. The remark survives through later RAI broadcasts and should be tied to this particular interview.'],['A tool of the trade','Amphetamine could suppress tiredness and hunger and create a feeling of surplus energy. That was precisely what made it dangerous: the body’s warnings grew weaker while the load continued.'],['Before controls','There was still no effective international testing system. This does not mean that use was harmless or universally accepted, but that the boundary between medicine, stimulant and cheating was handled far more loosely.'],['A remark with another side','Coppi’s dry answer can sound amusing. Historically, it matters more as a rare open window into a working culture in which riders pushed the body without the safeguards later rules attempted to create.']], sources:[['RAI: Fausto e la bomba','https://www.raiplay.it/video/2019/07/Coppi100-Fausto-e-la-bomba-592bfdb3-8643-4dd3-8e95-bb0952d21807.html']] },
  'soigneuren-og-maden-i-1970erne': { year:'The 1970s', label:'Nutrition, training and science · The people behind cycling', title:'Steak at five and coffee with cognac', intro:'Before gels and precise carbohydrate plans, the soigneur packed sandwiches, fruit and tarts, filled the bottles and tried to keep riders eating through a long working day.', alt:'The Molteni team with Eddy Merckx during the team time trial at the 1971 Tour de France', caption:'The Molteni team during the team time trial at the 1971 Tour de France. The photograph shows the period’s team organisation, not a feed zone. Photograph: Anefo · CC BY-SA 3.0 NL.', sections:[['Before the riders came down to breakfast','The soigneur began work before the riders. Bottles had to be filled, small sandwiches prepared and the day’s musettes distributed. The role covered much more than massage. Soigneurs also handled clothing, drinks, food and much of the practical order around the team.'],['Steak and rice before sunrise','Breakfast could be heavy by modern standards. Steak and rice were regarded as reliable fuel and eaten several hours before the start. In 1977, Eddy Merckx said that for years he had eaten steak with rice at five or six in the morning and had reached the point where he could no longer face it. André Dierickx remembered split-stage days when riders might be served steak three times.'],['The bag at the roadside','In the feed zone, soigneurs held cloth bags at shoulder height. The rider had to find the right person, catch the strap without pulling the bag into the wheel and quickly move the contents into jersey pockets. A musette might contain small sandwiches, fruit, sugar, tarts or other food that could be eaten while riding. Contents varied between teams, races and riders; there was no single recipe for the whole peloton.'],['Sweet coffee and something stronger','Strong sweet coffee was common in cycling provisions, and accounts from the period also mention small mixtures of coffee and cognac. Alcohol was neither a precise fuelling plan nor a sound treatment for cramp. It belonged to a working culture in which experience, habit and anecdote carried more weight than modern sports nutrition. Cola was also used as a quick source of sugar, often after some of the carbonation had been released.'],['When the musette was missed','A dropped musette could be costly, especially when the next opportunity for food lay far ahead. The rider was not necessarily left without help; under certain conditions, rules allowed bottles and food from the team car. Yet too little food during a long race could still bring the feared fringale, the hunger crisis in which pace, concentration and judgement disappeared almost at once.'],['Between craft and science','The 1970s were not a decade without knowledge. Teams learned from experience, and some worked more systematically than others. Nutrition, however, was rarely planned in grams of carbohydrate per hour. The soigneur’s essential task was more ordinary: know the riders, prepare the food and make sure it was actually eaten. That required no laboratory. It required an alarm clock, a knife and a considerable amount of white bread.']], sources:[['KOERS Museum: Jean-Pierre Monseré – steak as a source of strength','https://servicekoers.be/verhalen/jean-pierre-monsere-biefstuk'],['KOERS Museum: La Grande Bouffe – feeding during the Tour','https://servicekoers.be/verhalen/bevoorrading-tijdens-de-tour'],['KOERS Museum: Briek Schotte – bread in the musette','https://servicekoers.be/verhalen/briek-schotte-brood'],['Wikimedia Commons: Molteni team, Tour de France 1971','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Molteni,_Team_time_trial_prologue,_1971_Tour_de_France.jpg']] },
} as const;

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return ['circuit-de-france-1942','gino-bartali-kurer-under-krigen','fausto-coppi-fra-krigsfange-til-comeback','jean-robic-vender-touren-1947','campagnolo-gran-sport-parallelogram','roger-walkowiak-tour-1956','charly-gaul-monte-bondone-1956','tour-start-amsterdam-1954','fausto-coppi-la-bomba','soigneuren-og-maden-i-1970erne'].map(slug => ({ params:{ slug } }));
}
const slug = Astro.params.slug as keyof typeof copy;
const story = copy[slug];
const meta = englishStories.find(item => item.slug === slug)!;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const imageSrc = typeof meta.image === 'string' ? `${base}/${meta.image.replace(/^\//, '')}` : meta.image.src;
const canonical = `https://teob.dk/en/stories/${slug}/`;
const danish = `/historier/${slug}/`;
const period = slug === 'soigneuren-og-maden-i-1970erne' ? '1970-1979' : ['campagnolo-gran-sport-parallelogram','roger-walkowiak-tour-1956','charly-gaul-monte-bondone-1956','tour-start-amsterdam-1954','fausto-coppi-la-bomba'].includes(slug) ? '1950-1959' : '1940-1949';
const schema = {'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'Article',headline:story.title,description:story.intro,inLanguage:'en',mainEntityOfPage:canonical,publisher:{'@type':'Organization',name:'The Evolution of Bikes',url:'https://teob.dk/'}};
---
<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><title>{story.title} · The Evolution of Bikes</title><meta name="description" content={story.intro}/><link rel="canonical" href={canonical}/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="da" href={`https://teob.dk${danish}`}/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href={canonical}/><meta property="og:type" content="article"/><meta property="og:locale" content="en_GB"/><meta property="og:title" content={story.title}/><meta property="og:description" content={story.intro}/><meta property="og:url" content={canonical}/><meta property="og:image" content={imageSrc.startsWith('http') ? imageSrc : `https://teob.dk${imageSrc}`}/><script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)}/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body>
<EnglishHeader current="stories" danishPath={danish}/><main><article class="track-page"><header class="track-hero"><p class="section-label">{story.label} · {story.year}</p><h1>{story.title}</h1><p>{story.intro}</p></header><figure class="hero-image"><img src={imageSrc} alt={story.alt} loading="eager"/><figcaption>{story.caption}</figcaption></figure><div class="story-copy"><p class="lead">{story.intro}</p>{story.sections.map(([heading,text])=><section><h2>{heading}</h2><p>{text}</p></section>)}</div><section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and notes</p><div><p>The article distinguishes documented events from details preserved in later accounts.</p><ul>{story.sources.map(([label,url])=><li><a href={url}>{label}</a></li>)}</ul></div></section><nav class="era-bottom-nav" aria-label="Further navigation"><a href={`${base}/en/stories/`}>← All stories</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/${period}/`}>The period →</a></nav></article></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
<style>.track-page{padding-top:3rem}.track-hero{max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;padding:4rem 1.5rem 3rem}.track-hero h1{font-family:'Libre Caslon Display',serif;font-size:clamp(3rem,7vw,6.4rem);font-weight:400;line-height:1;margin:.6rem 0 1.5rem}.track-hero>p:last-child{max-width:760px;font-size:1.25rem;line-height:1.65}.hero-image{max-width:1180px;margin:0 auto 4rem}.hero-image>img{width:100%;max-height:760px;object-fit:cover}.hero-image figcaption{font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.55;color:#625e55;margin-top:.75rem}.story-copy{max-width:760px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1.5rem 4rem;font-size:1.08rem;line-height:1.82}.story-copy .lead{font-family:'Libre Caslon Display',serif;font-size:1.7rem;line-height:1.5}.story-copy h2{font-family:'Libre Caslon Display',serif;font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem);font-weight:400;line-height:1.15;margin:3rem 0 1rem}.period-sources{max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto}.period-sources li{overflow-wrap:anywhere}@media(max-width:800px){.track-page{padding-top:0}.track-hero{padding-top:2.5rem}.hero-image figcaption{padding:0 1.25rem}.story-copy{font-size:1rem}.story-copy .lead{font-size:1.4rem}}</style>
